1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,480
In this video, you will learn why octopuses

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:04,960
have three hearts, why their blood is blue,

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:07,360
and how this anatomy helps them survive in

4
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:08,400
cold, deep water.

5
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:11,920
The answer is clever natural engineering. Two

6
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:14,320
of the hearts, called branchial hearts, pump

7
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:16,726
blood through the gills to collect oxygen. The

8
00:00:16,726 --> 00:00:18,966
third, the systemic heart, pushes that

9
00:00:18,966 --> 00:00:21,846
oxygen-rich blood around the body. Each pump

10
00:00:21,846 --> 00:00:24,326
has exactly one job, and together they keep

11
00:00:24,326 --> 00:00:26,886
the octopus alive. Here is the surprising

12
00:00:26,886 --> 00:00:29,606
part: when an octopus swims, its systemic

13
00:00:29,606 --> 00:00:32,130
heart stops beating. Swimming exhausts the

14
00:00:32,130 --> 00:00:34,690
animal, because less oxygen reaches its body.

15
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:37,570
That is one reason octopuses usually crawl

16
00:00:37,570 --> 00:00:39,970
along the seafloor. Their blood is blue

17
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:43,130
because it carries oxygen with hemocyanin, a

18
00:00:43,130 --> 00:00:45,250
copper-based protein, not the iron that makes

19
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:48,359
our blood red. When hemocyanin binds oxygen,

20
00:00:48,359 --> 00:00:50,759
it turns blue, and it works well in cold,

21
00:00:50,839 --> 00:00:53,879
oxygen-poor water. So the takeaway is simple:

22
00:00:54,119 --> 00:00:56,759
two hearts feed the gills, one feeds the body,

23
00:00:56,999 --> 00:00:59,079
and copper-based blue blood moves oxygen

24
00:00:59,079 --> 00:01:01,239
efficiently in the cold deep. A strange

25
00:01:01,239 --> 00:01:03,799
design, but perfectly suited to the octopus's

26
00:01:03,799 --> 00:01:04,119
world.
